    "title": "OrbVM: Topological Phase Memory for Distributed Coherence",
    "doi": "10.5281/zenodo.19058639",
    "publication_date": "2026-03-17",
    "description": "<p>We present OrbVM, a Rust-based virtual machine implementing topological phase memory through co-evolution of dispersive wave dynamics and Kuramoto synchronization. The<br>architecture combines Yang-Baxter invariants for fault detection, White&rsquo;s fourth-order dispersion equation for phase memory stability, and distributed oscillator coupling for emergent<br>consensus. We demonstrate integration with the Digital Memory Ring (DMR) substrate,<br>achieving 40&ndash;170 ms end-to-end latency for coherence computation and 23.8 Orbs/second<br>throughput on commodity hardware. The system exhibits critical phenomena at coupling<br>strength K &asymp; 0.618 (golden ratio) and coherence threshold &lambda;2 &asymp; 0.847, with convergence<br>time scaling as &tau; &sim; N1/2<br>for N nodes. Independent architectural convergences with recent photonic beam-scanning hardware (Saha et al., Nature 2026), topological molecular<br>switches (IBM 2024), and quantum teleportation networks (Oxford 2025) suggest broader<br>applicability. Full source code and experimental validation are provided.</p>",
